{"agent_id":"pp3g-rne6-fast-re-pass-of-revised-plan-v2-on-341-read-only-hy3","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-10T01:38:16.774919670Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"be776be6-96c3-4686-8772-ececc379dacf","comment_uuid":"6193b2a6-0277-4aac-993c-2adf43812d5d","author":"pp3g-rne6-fast-re-pass-of-revised-plan-v2-on-341-read-only-hy3","content":"=== hy3 FAST RE-PASS on REVISED PLAN v2 — VERDICT: MAKE CHANGES (narrow) ===\n\nScope: v2 only. R1-R6 checked one by one against the v2 text and against the working tree wherever v2 makes a new factual claim. Read-only; working tree untouched.\n\nHEADLINE: all six required changes are ACCEPTED. The v1 near-showstoppers are gone — nothing in v2 is destructive, and the inverted classification that could have reverted live governance docs is correctly reversed. The verdict is MAKE CHANGES only because v2 introduces/retains six SECOND-ORDER defects, four of which are provable and two of which make v2's own acceptance criteria unsatisfiable by any step it defines. These are the same defect class I flagged as R3 in v1 (an AC no step can reach), so consistency requires flagging them. Every one is a scoped text fix; none requires re-planning.\n\n--- R1-R6 VERIFICATION (all PASS) ---\nR1 PASS. A2 correctly reclassified as SUPERSEDED pre-filing drafts; 'staged adds frontmatter + restructure' struck; 'prefer staged-with-frontmatter' explicitly disapplied; committed versions declared authoritative. The destructive path is closed.\nR2 PASS. B1 = re-add YAML to committed Evaluation Framework, keep committed body. Re-verified: committed 338 lines vs staged 367, delta 29 — consistent with the 30-line YAML block minus one blank. Mechanical, as required. B2 = pick committed ordering, record, move on. Mechanical.\nR3 PASS. AC2 now reads 'resolves to a committed file OR a recorded, issue-tracked gap', with the registry gap named and carried as D4. Re-verified against ORIENTATION: it lists EIGHT canonical docs (v1 said nine — v1's count was the imprecise one, v2 does not repeat it). Five resolve today, C1/C2/C3 close three, leaving exactly one residual gap (AI Capability Registry — only the Specification is committed). AC2 is now satisfiable.\nR4 PASS on all seven sub-points. harness-evaluations and selection-rationale stay Research; capability-mapping split (Harness-Capability-Matrix Research / Model-Routing-Matrix operational); core-system-prompts argued explicitly and escalated to D2; 'methodology-support' and 'decision records' dropped as layer values; _template.md excluded; metadata-first with git mv deferred to 4b as a separate operator-approved issue. Counts re-verified on disk and v2's arithmetic is RIGHT: harness-evaluations 5 minus template = 4, selection-rationale 3 minus template = 2, capability-mapping 2, core-system-prompts 2. (Only two _template.md exist in these four dirs; the third is in methodology-reviews/, out of scope. v2 handled this correctly.)\nR5 PASS in substance. Malformed YAML repair scoped to C4/C5/C6 — re-verified, all three use markdown asterisk bullets under depends_on/consumed_by and a stray blank line after the opening '---'. programme->program normalisation present. C4 gated on operator decision D1 with C7 held as its decision-record input. Correctly, C3 is NOT in the normalisation list because it already uses 'program:' — v2 got that detail right.\nR6 PASS. scripts/ is in the sweep scope, alongside CHANGELOG, .design/, research-addenda/, knowledge pages, ORIENTATION.md, ARCHITECTURE.md. Sweep folded per-file into the same commit as the move (Step 1f), with Step 4a reduced to navigation. Re-verified the mechanism: scripts/validate_evaluation.py detect_doc_type matches on path PARTS, so 4b relocation survives provided the directory NAME is preserved.\n\n--- STILL WRONG (new/residual only — no repeats) ---\n\nS1. MUST FIX — the canonical_repository mirror note is applied to C3 only, but C1 and C2 need it too.\n  Verified frontmatter: ai-orchestration-guide.md -> canonical_repository: ases. methodology-to-requirements-mapping-spec.md -> canonical_repository: ases. execution-engine-vision.md -> canonical_repository: ases-engine.\n  ALL THREE declare an authoritative repository other than edases. v2's caveat-2a treatment covers only C3. Filing C1 and C2 into this repo without the same explicit mirror/placeholder note reproduces exactly the contradiction the note exists to prevent (Documentation Standard, canonical_repository: 'Identifies the authoritative repository'). This matters most for C1: the AI Orchestration Guide is the anchor whose presence is what gives docs/methodology/ its semantics, and it would land claiming its real home is elsewhere. Fix: extend the 2a note to C1 and C2, or escalate 'edases hosts three docs canonical to ases/ases-engine' as an operator decision.\n  NOTE: v1 missed this too — I named only C3. It is still wrong in v2, so it is in scope.\n\nS2. MUST FIX — programme->program normalisation is under-scoped to C1/C2, but C5 and C6 also carry 'programme:'.\n  Verified: SKILL-clean-room-execution (C4), edases-methodology-review (C5), repo-review-checklist (C6) all use 'programme:'. C5 and C6 are filed by Step 1 in the same pass as C1/C2. As written, one commit would file two docs spelled 'program:' and two spelled 'programme:'. Fix: normalisation scope = C1, C2, C5, C6, and C4 if routed — i.e. every file Step 1 touches.\n\nS3. MUST FIX — AC3 is unsatisfiable because Step 3a/3b instruct a no-op.\n  Verified: NONE of the 12 tool-capability files has any YAML frontmatter (git grep '^layer:' returns zero hits across all four dirs; they use bold-key markdown headers). 3a says 'KEEP layer Research ... NO layer change. Fix any layer/document_type metadata drift only.' There is no metadata, therefore no drift, therefore 3a is literally a no-op — yet AC3 requires 'layer metadata on harness-evaluations/, selection-rationale/, capability-mapping/, core-system-prompts/ correct per the 5 permitted layer values.' 3c ('update frontmatter accordingly') and 3d ('apply metadata only') do imply authoring; 3a and 3b do not. Fix: restate 3a/3b as AUTHOR frontmatter (none exists) with layer: Research, rather than 'fix drift'. This is also an effort-estimate correction — Step 3 is authoring 12 headers from scratch, not editing existing ones.\n\nS4. MUST FIX — AC5 is unsatisfiable: seven staged files are never removed by any step.\n  Staging holds 18 files. v2 removes 4 (A2 cleanup) and moves 5 out (C1, C2, C3, C5, C6), leaving 9. AC5 says staging should reduce to 'only unresolved items (C4/C7 pending)' — i.e. 2. The 7-file shortfall is the five Category-A1 byte-identical duplicates plus the B1 and B2 staged copies. The Category A header calls them 'safe to drop', but that is a classification statement; Step 2's cleanup instruction names A2 only. Fix: add A1 and the spent B1/B2 staged copies to the Step 2 cleanup commit, or relax AC5 to enumerate exactly what remains.\n\nS5. MUST FIX (small) — a generated artefact is inside Step 3's scope.\n  harness-evaluations/Microsoft-AutoGen.md.trace.md is one of the four non-template files 3a would touch. Its own header reads 'Auto-generated by scripts/validate_evaluation.py --emit-trace ... Do not hand-edit', and emit_evidence_trace rebuilds the file from an empty list and write_text's it wholesale — any frontmatter authored into it is silently WIPED on the next --emit-trace run. Editing it also contradicts Documentation Standard, Authority Rules: 'Generated documents should never be edited directly.' Fix: exclude *.trace.md exactly as 3f excludes _template.md, or classify it authority: Generated and leave it alone. Effective harness-evaluations authoring scope is 3 files, not 4.\n\nS6. SHOULD CONSIDER — 'Implementation/Tooling' is used as a layer value in 3c, 3d and 4b, which contradicts 3e.\n  Verified against Documentation Standard: layer permits exactly Research / Methodology / Requirements / Architecture / Implementation. 'Tooling' is a PROGRAM value (permitted: EDASES / ASES / Tooling), not a layer. 3e correctly forbids inventing layer values, then 3c/3d/4b reintroduce one — the same conflation R4 removed, wearing a different token. Fix: say layer: Implementation, program: Tooling. Non-blocking because the intent is unambiguous, but it will propagate into the authored frontmatter if left.\n\nS7. SHOULD CONSIDER — the sweep covers PATH references but not TITLE references, so the relationship graph breaks on arrival.\n  The same document is named three ways across the set being filed: ORIENTATION says 'Methodology -> Requirements Specification', C1's consumed_by says 'Methodology -> Requirements Mapping', C3's depends_on says 'Methodology -> Requirements Specification', and C2's own title is 'Methodology to Requirements Mapping Specification'. R6's sweep scope and AC4 ('no broken PATH references') do not reach depends_on/consumed_by title strings. Documentation Standard, Relationship Graph, expects the graph to 'permit automated validation'. Related: C1's depends_on cites 'AI Capability Registry', which is precisely the AC2 gap — filing C1 creates a canonical doc with a dangling dependency on a document that does not exist. D4 records the gap, which softens this, but the plan should say the dangling edge is knowingly accepted. Fix: extend the sweep to title references in depends_on/consumed_by/related_documents, or add an AC allowing known-dangling edges only where issue-tracked.\n\nS8. NIT — two document_type values drift from the standard's list without comment: C3 uses 'Vision' where the standard lists 'Architecture Vision'; C5 uses 'Methodology' where 'Methodology Specification' exists. The standard permits new types, so this is legal, but Step 1's 'verify frontmatter completeness' is the natural place to decide.\n\nS9. NIT — 'programme:' is not just a staging problem: nine ALREADY-COMMITTED docs use it, including Canonical Terminology, Concept - Levels of Abstraction and Documentation Taxonomy. Fixing only the newly-filed files is defensible scoping, but 'normalise programme -> program' currently reads like a repo-wide claim the plan does not deliver. Either scope it out in one line or record it as an issue-tracked gap alongside D4. (v1's separate observation that Documentation Standard is itself internally inconsistent — example block line 86 says last_update, definition heading line 253 says last_updated — remains unaddressed and is the root cause worth fixing first.)\n\n--- DERISKED, NO ACTION NEEDED ---\nI re-tested the one risk v2's Step 3 could plausibly have introduced. Authoring YAML frontmatter into harness-evaluations files is SAFE against scripts/validate_evaluation.py: check_required_sections uses a plain substring test ('if section not in content'), and parse_observations/parse_findings match line-level **OBS-NN:** / **FIN-NN:** patterns that a YAML block cannot satisfy. No warning is introduced. Step 3 does not need to fear the validator.\n\n--- BOTTOM LINE ---\nv2 is a genuine, faithful revision — R1-R6 are all discharged, and the one item that could have destroyed live governance content is fixed correctly. S1-S5 are the blocking set and are all localised text edits. With those five applied, Step 1 (C1, C2, C3, C5, C6), Step 2 and Step 3 are safe to execute; C4/C7 stay correctly gated on D1.\n\n--- REASONING CERTAINTY ---\nWHY: v2's claims were re-checked against the working tree and the canonical standards rather than accepted from the issue text, because v1's single worst error was an unverified classification.\nWHAT: reads of Documentation Standard in full (permitted program/layer values, Authority Rules, Repository Independence, Relationship Graph) and scripts/validate_evaluation.py in full; frontmatter reads of C1-C6; directory listings of all four tool-capability dirs; repo-wide glob for _template.md; git grep of '^layer:' and '^programme:'; line counts of both Evaluation Framework copies; read of ORIENTATION's Canonical Documents section; read of the trace file header; git status.\nHOW CERTAIN: PROVEN for S1, S2, S3, S5, S6, S8, S9 and for the derisking result — all direct file evidence. PROVEN for S4 by arithmetic over v2's own step list (18 - 4 - 5 = 9 vs AC5's 2). EVIDENCE-BASED for S7 (rests on the four differing title strings plus the standard's automated-validation expectation). All R1-R6 PASS verdicts are text-comparison against v1's stated requirements, corroborated by tree checks where v2 asserted new facts.\nWHAT-NOT-TESTED: I did not execute validate_evaluation.py — the frontmatter-safety conclusion is read from the source's substring and regex logic, not from a run. I did not re-diff the staged top-level files against committed (R1 accepted on v1's already-proven evidence). I did not verify the ~80 reference count or that the sweep scope is exhaustive — only that title references fall outside it. I did not read core-system-prompts or Harness-Capability-Matrix in full, so I take no position on D2 beyond v2 having argued it as required. I did not assess whether the C1-C7 bodies are current. I read no issue other than #341.","created_at":"2026-08-10T01:38:16.766122444Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:fZPkJGlZ5iZCiY6DC7DZO9rWk2R9UxjF7w5eztK7cS4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgMX8ert6O1mMvbhL/GkIDm5eTZ3bTk5j3K2Btr44JrKwAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABApEa6ubsafM3PChtmSW41X7jwXzI3kwfLlLD7BZnV04tiBcLBsJrgy0EBhxWAJnzXRfp62yU9En6xGIMHSxytDA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:fZPkJGlZ5iZCiY6DC7DZO9rWk2R9UxjF7w5eztK7cS4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgMX8ert6O1mMvbhL/GkIDm5eTZ3bTk5j3K2Btr44JrKwAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQBYVZl/128ohaoHtacOAVjA5T4hc5mrRkHwwUwBEnLfFHJ3XzEBo7TPmunyvH9YyZJNftubDqD9S6c2GHGDI7Q4="}
